---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro'
import { ogPathFor } from '../../data/og'
import PromptCard from '../../components/PromptCard.astro'
import PostByline from '../../components/PostByline.astro'
import ShellCommands from '../../components/ShellCommands'
import { CodeBlock } from '../../components/ouro/code-block/code-block'
import { Card } from '../../components/ouro/card/card'
import { Kbd } from '../../components/ouro/kbd/kbd'

// One title everywhere (taste review r2, 2026-11-06): the post's own headline.
// "Grow an agent" is the workshop's name; it lives in the eyebrow.
const title = "Just give the model a keyboard | Ouro"
const description = "Build an agent in about 100 lines of TypeScript. Learn what an agent loop is and how agents actually work. A practical guide to building agents that read, think, act, and grow: from first principles to production."
const canonicalUrl = "https://ouroboros.bot/blog/build-ai-agent-from-scratch/"
const datePublished = "2026-03-01"
const dateModified = "2026-11-11"

const yt = "https://youtube.com/watch?v=YFjfBk8HI5o&t=9298"

// The one program the reader grows, and the finished copy the site keeps for catching up.
const file = "agent.ts"
const finished = "/agent-loop/agent.ts"

// What the reader types. The same in every shell, one line at a time, because
// Windows PowerShell 5 has no &&.
const makeFolder = "mkdir my-agent\ncd my-agent\ngit init"
const run = `node ${file}`

const promptSetup = `We're going to build a tiny agent in TypeScript, in this folder, one step at a time. First, get the folder ready.

Check that Node is version 22.18 or later, and that the gh CLI is installed and signed in to an account with GitHub Copilot: gh api /copilot_internal/user should succeed. If anything's missing, tell me exactly how to fix it.

Then run npm init -y, npm pkg set type=module and npm install openai, and add a .gitignore with node_modules in it.

Commit when done. From now on, make small atomic commits as you work, and keep your work committed.`

const promptM1 = `We're building a tiny agent in TypeScript, in one file, ${file}, that grows step by step. Use only the openai package: no agent SDK, no other dependencies.

For the model, use my GitHub Copilot access through the gh CLI. When the program starts, get the token with gh auth token and the address with gh api /copilot_internal/user --jq .endpoints.api (use execFileSync), then create the client with new OpenAI({ apiKey, baseURL }). If gh fails, print "Couldn't reach GitHub Copilot through the gh CLI. Run: gh auth login" and exit. Use the model claude-sonnet-5.5.

I'll run it with node ${file}. Node runs .ts files by stripping the types, so stick to erasable TypeScript (no enums), with no build step and no tsconfig.

Now write ${file}: send one chat completions request with a single user message, "What is the secret word in secret.txt?", and print the reply. Claude can split one reply across several choices, so join the text from every item in response.choices, not just the first. Keep it short. Commit when done.`

const promptM2 = `Change ${file} into a chat in the terminal.

Keep a messages array. Read a line from me with node:readline, using the prompt "you> ". Push it as a user message, send the whole array, and print the reply as "agent> " followed by the text. Join the text across all choices, as before. Push the reply as an assistant message, then ask me again. Skip empty lines. I'll leave with Ctrl+C. Commit when done.`

const promptM3 = `Keep the chat in ${file}, and pass a tools array with one tool: read_file, described as "Read a text file and return its contents.", taking one string argument, path.

Join the text across all choices, and collect tool_calls from all choices too, not just the first. For each tool call, print "The model asked for a tool call:" and the raw call as pretty JSON. Then really read the file, and print We ran read_file("<path>") and got: followed by what you read.

Don't send the result back to the model yet. Print "...but we never sent the result back, so the model never answers. That's milestone 4." and leave the tool call out of messages, so the chat keeps working. Commit when done.`

const promptM4 = `Now make ${file} answer its tool calls, in a loop.

Tools: read_file, as before; write_file, taking path and content, described as "Write text to a file, replacing it if it exists."; and shell, taking one command string. Run shell commands with powershell.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -Command on Windows and /bin/sh -c everywhere else, and name that shell in the tool's description so the model writes the right syntax. On Windows, start each command with try { [Console]::OutputEncoding = [Text.Encoding]::UTF8 } catch {}; so the output comes back as UTF-8, with accents and emoji intact.

After each of my messages, call the model. Join the text and collect tool_calls across all choices. Push the assistant message, with its tool_calls, and print its text as "agent> " if there is any. If there are no tool calls, wait for me. Otherwise run each tool, print a line like "  [read_file] {"path":"secret.txt"}" instead of last step's printout, push each result as a tool message with its tool_call_id, and call the model again.

Keep it safe: at most 20 tool rounds per turn; a 30-second timeout on shell commands; tool output capped at 10 KB; JSON.parse of the arguments guarded, so bad JSON comes back to the model as an error string; unknown tools answered with an error string; and a non-zero exit code reported to the model along with the output.

Add a --show-messages flag that prints the messages array as JSON after each turn. Commit when done.`

const promptName = `Read ${file}. That's your own source code. Pick a name for yourself, then change the "agent> " label to it. Commit, and tell me when to restart.`

const growList = `Add a list_directory tool to yourself. Read ${file}, follow the pattern of the tools already there, add the definition and the handler, and write the file. Commit when done, then tell me how to test it.`

const growStream = `Make yourself nicer to use. Stream your replies: pass stream: true and print the text as it arrives, rebuilding the text and tool_calls from the deltas of every choice. Show a small spinner on stderr until the first chunk arrives, with no new dependencies. Catch API errors, print them clearly and go back to the prompt instead of crashing. Commit when done.`

const growRegistry = `Your tools are getting crowded. Refactor them into a registry: one object that maps each tool name to its definition and its handler. Running a tool should just look it up. Keep everything else working the same. Commit when done.`

const growPurpose = `Add a system message at the start of messages. Before you write it, ask me questions, one per turn, about who you should be and how I like to work. Make sure you know you're a coding agent with file and shell tools, and that ${file} is your own code. Introduce yourself when you start. Commit when done.`

const growSkills = `Build a skills system. Make a skills folder with two example skills as markdown files, code-review.md and explain.md. Add a list_skills tool, and a load_skill tool that reads a skill and adds it to your system message. Commit when done.`

const growToolmaker = `Write a skill called toolmaker.md that teaches you how to add a tool to yourself: the definition, the handler, and a reminder to tell me to restart. Then load it and use it to add a get_current_time tool. Commit when done.`

const promptByok = `Switch ${file} from GitHub Copilot to my own provider. I'll tell you which one, its address and the model. Load my key from a .env file with process.loadEnvFile(), make sure .env is in .gitignore, and tell me what to put in it. Keep everything else the same. Commit when done.`

const promptResponses = `Copy ${file} to agent-responses.ts and switch it to client.responses.create, with the model I give you. Keep the same tools and safety limits. Keep the conversation as a list of input items, append each response's output items, run every function_call item, and send back a function_call_output item with the matching call_id, until a round has no function calls. Commit when done.`

// What the reader's terminal shows at each step. The program's own lines come
// from the finished agent.ts; the model's words are examples.
const outM1 = `$ ${run}
I don't have access to your files, so I can't see what's in secret.txt. If you paste its contents here, I'm happy to help.`

const outM2 = `$ ${run}
you> my name is Sam
agent> Nice to meet you, Sam!
you> what's my name?
agent> Your name is Sam.
you> what's the secret word in secret.txt?
agent> I can't open files, so I don't know what's in secret.txt.
you> `

const outM3 = String.raw`$ ${run}
you> what's the secret word in secret.txt?
The model asked for a tool call:
{
  "id": "call_…",
  "type": "function",
  "function": {
    "name": "read_file",
    "arguments": "{\"path\":\"secret.txt\"}"
  }
}
We ran read_file("secret.txt") and got:
pineapple
...but we never sent the result back, so the model never answers. That's milestone 4.
you> `

const outM4 = String.raw`$ ${run}
you> what's the secret word in secret.txt?
  [read_file] {"path":"secret.txt"}
agent> The secret word is "pineapple".
you> write a haiku to hello.txt
  [write_file] {"path":"hello.txt","content":"Keys under my hands\nI read, I write, I run things\nThe loop carries on\n"}
agent> Done. I wrote a haiku to hello.txt.
you> `

const outMessages = String.raw`$ ${run} --show-messages
you> what's the secret word in secret.txt?
  [read_file] {"path":"secret.txt"}
agent> The secret word is "pineapple".
[
  {
    "role": "user",
    "content": "what's the secret word in secret.txt?"
  },
  {
    "role": "assistant",
    "content": "",
    "tool_calls": [
      {
        "id": "call_…",
        "type": "function",
        "function": {
          "name": "read_file",
          "arguments": "{\"path\":\"secret.txt\"}"
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "tool",
    "tool_call_id": "call_…",
    "content": "pineapple\n"
  },
  {
    "role": "assistant",
    "content": "The secret word is \"pineapple\"."
  }
]
you> `

const outName = `$ ${run}
you> Read ${file}. That's your own source code. Pick a name for yourself…
  [read_file] {"path":"${file}"}
  [write_file] {"path":"${file}","content":"…"}
  [shell] {"command":"git commit -am \\"Name myself Pip\\""}
agent> I'm Pip now. Press Ctrl+C and run me again to meet me.
you> `

const loopCode = `for (let round = 1; round <= 20; round++) {
  const reply = await askModel(messages, tools)
  messages.push(reply)
  if (!reply.tool_calls) return   // no tool calls: it's done
  for (const call of reply.tool_calls) {
    messages.push(runTool(call))   // hand back the result
  }
}`

const termSkills = `you> list skills
you> load code-review, then review ${file}`

const faq = [
  {
    q: "What is an agent loop?",
    a: "An agent loop is the cycle that makes an AI agent work: the model reads the conversation, decides on an action (like reading a file or running a command), your program runs it and hands back the result, and the model decides again. It repeats until the model answers without asking for anything. The loop turns a model that can only talk into an agent that can act.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do AI agents work?",
    a: "An AI agent is a language model with tools, inside a loop. The model can't run anything itself; it asks for a tool call, the program runs it and adds the result to the conversation, and the model goes again. Three tools, read file, write file and run shell, let an agent do almost anything a developer can do at a terminal, including change its own code.",
  },
  {
    q: "How many lines of code does it take to build an AI agent?",
    a: `The loop itself fits on one screen. The finished ${file} in this guide, with three tools and safety limits, is about 100 lines of TypeScript. Streaming, memory, skills and everything else grow from there, often written by the agent itself.`,
  },
  {
    q: "What tools does an AI agent need?",
    a: "At minimum, three: read file, write file and run a shell command. With file access and a shell, a model can install packages, run tests, call APIs and change code. You can add more later, and the agent can add them to itself.",
  },
]

const exampleNote = "Example: the model's words will vary."
const outputExempt = "program output, shown as evidence"
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} ogImage={ogPathFor('/blog/build-ai-agent-from-scratch')} canonical={canonicalUrl} ogType="article">

  <Fragment slot="head">
    <meta property="article:published_time" content={datePublished} />
    <meta property="article:modified_time" content={dateModified} />
    <meta property="article:author" content="Ari Mendelow" />
    <meta property="article:tag" content="AI agents" />
    <meta property="article:tag" content="agent loop" />
    <meta property="article:tag" content="TypeScript" />
    <meta name="author" content="Ari Mendelow" />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "TechArticle",
      "headline": "Just give the model a keyboard",
      "description": description,
      "author": {
        "@type": "Person",
        "name": "Ari Mendelow",
        "url": "https://github.com/arimendelow"
      },
      "publisher": {
        "@type": "Organization",
        "name": "Ouro Labs",
        "url": "https://ouroboros.bot",
        "logo": {
          "@type": "ImageObject",
          "url": "https://ouroboros.bot/images/ouroboros.png"
        }
      },
      "datePublished": datePublished,
      "dateModified": dateModified,
      "mainEntityOfPage": canonicalUrl,
      "keywords": ["AI agent", "agent loop", "how to build an AI agent", "what is an agent loop", "how do AI agents work", "TypeScript agent", "LLM agent", "agentic AI", "workshop"],
      "inLanguage": "en",
      "isAccessibleForFree": true
    })} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "mainEntity": faq.map(({ q, a }) => ({
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": q,
        "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": a }
      }))
    })} />
    <!-- The reader's shell, before anything paints: saved choice, else a guess from the platform.
         Without JavaScript nothing is set, so every variant shows under its own caption. -->
    <script is:inline>
      try {
        var saved = localStorage.getItem('ouroboros-agent-loop-shell');
        document.documentElement.dataset.shellActive = ['mac', 'powershell', 'wsl'].indexOf(saved) >= 0
          ? saved
          : navigator.userAgent.indexOf('Windows') >= 0 ? 'powershell' : 'mac';
      } catch (e) {}
    </script>
  </Fragment>

  <article class="px-6 lg:px-16 pt-32 pb-20">
      <!-- Header -->
      <header class="max-w-6xl mb-12 md:mb-8 mx-auto split lg:items-end">
        <div class="split-head">
          <span class="text-xs font-mono text-fang uppercase tracking-[0.22em]">Grow an agent</span>
          <h1 class="font-display text-4xl md:text-5xl lg:text-6xl text-bone leading-tight mt-4">
            Just give the model a keyboard
          </h1>
        </div>
        <div class="split-body">
          <p class="text-base md:text-lg text-mist mt-5 leading-relaxed">
            How to build an agent from scratch, understand what an agent loop really is, and grow something that modifies its own code.
          </p>
          <PostByline authors={["Ari Mendelow"]} published={datePublished} updated={dateModified} />
        </div>
      </header>

    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto">

      <div class="prose-ouro">

        <p><strong class="text-bone">The loop fits on one screen, and it isn't the clever part.</strong></p>
        <p>
          Every coding agent you've used, the ones that read your files, run your tests and fix their own mistakes,
          sits on a loop short enough to read in one breath. The model is the clever part. The loop just keeps handing
          it the keyboard.
        </p>
        <p>
          The people who know this best say everyone should build one at some point. <a href={yt}>Peter Steinberger</a>,
          who built OpenClaw, put it this way:
        </p>
        <blockquote>It's like the Hello World in AI, you know? And it's actually quite simple.</blockquote>
        <p>
          That's why I first built one. Today you write that Hello World. Within the hour you'll have an agent that
          reads files, runs commands and edits its own code, and you'll have watched every piece of it arrive.
        </p>

        <h2>Why take the long way</h2>
        <p>
          Most days I don't give my agents steps. I give them the outcome I want and trust them with the rest. That's
          <a href="/approach/">how I work</a>, and <a href="/blog/trust-the-agent/">why it works</a>. You could do that
          here too: tell any coding agent "make me an agent loop" and you'd have one in a minute.
        </p>
        <p>
          You'd also have a file you don't understand. So today we take the long way, one small step at a time, until
          the loop stops being magic and becomes yours. A coding agent still writes every line. You steer.
        </p>
        <p>
          The long way still isn't the manual way. Your coding agent checks your setup, installs packages and commits
          its own work, because you ask it for the outcome. Typing those yourself would be the first of
          <a href="/approach/#smells">the smells</a>: doing it by hand.
        </p>

        <h2 id="before-you-start">Before we start</h2>
        <p>
          All you need is a coding agent you like, working on a machine you can use: Copilot CLI, Claude Code, Codex,
          any of them. For the model, we'll use the GitHub Copilot access you already have: your agent wires it up
          through the <code>gh</code> CLI.
        </p>
        <p>Make a folder for your agent and make it a git repo. Type these one line at a time:</p>
        <CodeBlock client:load code={makeFolder} language="shell" title="terminal" wrap />
        <p>
          Your agent will soon run any command it likes in here, so keep nothing secret in this folder. Start your
          coding agent in it, and tell it:
        </p>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your coding agent" prompt={promptSetup} /></div>
        <p>
          It'll check your setup, initialize npm, install dependencies and commit. That's its job. Every prompt from
          here ends the same way. One small commit per step means you can see exactly what each step changed, and undo
          any of them. Your agent keeps that history for you.
        </p>
        <p>You're ready.</p>

        <div id="troubleshooting" class="trouble my-6" role="region" aria-label="If something goes wrong" tabindex="0">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">You see</th><th scope="col">Try</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>An error that mentions <code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code></td>
                <td>The program isn't getting its token from <code>gh</code>. Ask your coding agent to build the client from <code>gh auth token</code>, as the first prompt says.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>401</code></td>
                <td>Your sign-in has lapsed. Run <code>gh auth login</code>, then run your agent again.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>403</code></td>
                <td>This account has no Copilot. Switch to one that does with <code>gh auth switch</code>.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>400</code>, model not supported</td>
                <td>Your plan doesn't offer that model. Ask your coding agent to use <code>gpt-4.1</code> instead.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>400</code>, not accessible through chat completions</td>
                <td>That model only speaks the newer Responses API. Pick another model, or take the <a href="#responses-api">Responses side quest</a>.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>429</code>, quota exceeded</td>
                <td>That account has used its Copilot quota. Switch to one that hasn't with <code>gh auth switch</code>.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>gh: command not found</code></td>
                <td>Ask your coding agent to install the GitHub CLI, then run <code>gh auth login</code>. WSL needs its own.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>PowerShell rejects <code>&amp;&amp;</code></td>
                <td>Windows PowerShell has no <code>&amp;&amp;</code>. Run the commands one line at a time.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <aside id="byok-signpost" class="post-note my-8">
          <p class="!mb-0 !text-base">
            <strong class="text-bone">Side quest: bring your own key.</strong> Rather use MiniMax, Azure OpenAI or
            OpenAI? <a href="#bring-your-own-key">Tell your coding agent your provider.</a>
          </p>
        </aside>

        <h2>Plant a secret</h2>
        <p>Your agent needs something to find. Hide a word in a file. Pick your shell, and any word you like:</p>
        <ShellCommands client:load mac="echo pineapple > secret.txt" powershell="Set-Content secret.txt pineapple" />
        <p>Remember it. In a minute you'll ask the model for it, and it won't have a clue.</p>

        <h2 id="m1">Milestone 1: first contact</h2>
        <p>
          Start with the smallest thing that could work: send the model one message and print what comes back. This
          first prompt also tells your coding agent how the whole project fits together, so the next three go quickly.
        </p>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your coding agent" prompt={promptM1} /></div>
        <p id="m1-try">Try it:</p>
        <CodeBlock client:load code={run} language="shell" title="terminal" />
        <CodeBlock client:load code={outM1} language="text" title="What you'll see" copy={false} data-copy-exempt={outputExempt} data-expected-output="" wrap />
        <p class="output-note">{exampleNote}</p>
        <p>
          Behind, or want to check your work? Ask your coding agent to catch you up to the step you're on. The finished
          <a href={finished}>{file}</a> and its <a href="/agent-loop/package.json">package.json</a> are here too.
        </p>
        <p>
          The model answers, politely and confidently, and without your word. It might guess. It might apologize. It
          can't know: it has never seen your folder. All it has is the one message you sent. (If its reply opens with a
          <code>&lt;think&gt;</code> block, <a href="#think-tags">there's a side quest for that</a>.)
        </p>

        <h2 id="m2">Milestone 2: conversation</h2>
        <p>An agent that can't hold a conversation isn't an agent.</p>
        <p>
          Models are fixed: they don't learn, and each call knows nothing about the calls before it. What a model can do
          is take in a lot at once, everything in and out of one call, and that's its context window. So a chat is your
          program keeping a list of messages, called <code>messages</code>, and sending the whole list every time. Keep
          an eye on it.
        </p>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your coding agent" prompt={promptM2} /></div>
        <p>Try it. Tell it your name, then ask what your name is:</p>
        <CodeBlock client:load code={run} language="shell" title="terminal" />
        <CodeBlock client:load code={outM2} language="text" title="What you'll see" copy={false} data-copy-exempt={outputExempt} data-expected-output="" wrap />
        <p class="output-note">{exampleNote}</p>
        <p>
          It remembers. But it can't <em>do</em> anything yet. Ask for the secret word, and it still has no idea. Press
          <Kbd client:visible keys={["ctrl", "c"]} /> when you're done.
        </p>

        <h2 id="m3">Milestone 3: one tool</h2>
        <p>
          Time to give the model its first key. A tool is just a description: a name, a sentence about what it does,
          and the shape of its arguments. The model can't run anything. It can only ask. Here's what asking looks like.
        </p>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your coding agent" prompt={promptM3} /></div>
        <p>Try it, and ask for the secret word:</p>
        <CodeBlock client:load code={run} language="shell" title="terminal" />
        <CodeBlock client:load code={outM3} language="text" title="What you'll see" copy={false} data-copy-exempt={outputExempt} data-expected-output="" />
        <p class="output-note">{exampleNote}</p>
        <p>
          Look at that. The model knew it needed the file, named it, and asked for it in tidy JSON. Your program read
          it, and the word is right there on your screen. The model will never see it, because nobody told it.
        </p>
        <p><strong class="text-bone">It asked, and nobody answered.</strong></p>

        <h2 id="m4">Milestone 4: the loop</h2>
        <p>
          So answer it. Run the tool, add the result to <code>messages</code>, and call the model again. If it asks for
          another tool, do it again. When it stops asking, it's done. While you're there, give it two more keys: one to
          write files and one to run commands.
        </p>
        <p><strong class="text-bone">Read file + Write file + Run shell = universal basis.</strong></p>
        <p>With these three tools, a model can do almost anything a developer can do. Including modify its own code.</p>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your coding agent" prompt={promptM4} /></div>
        <p>Try it, and ask for the secret word:</p>
        <CodeBlock client:load code={run} language="shell" title="terminal" />
        <CodeBlock client:load code={outM4} language="text" title="What you'll see" copy={false} data-copy-exempt={outputExempt} data-expected-output="" />
        <p class="output-note">{exampleNote}</p>
        <p>
          This time it reaches for <code>read_file</code>, gets the word back, and tells you. Then ask for something you
          never planned: "write a haiku to hello.txt", "how many lines is {file}?", "what's in this folder?". It reads,
          writes, runs, checks its own work and answers.
        </p>
        <p>Now run it once more with the curtain open:</p>
        <CodeBlock client:load code={`${run} --show-messages`} language="shell" title="terminal" />
        <CodeBlock client:load code={outMessages} language="text" title="What you'll see" copy={false} data-copy-exempt={outputExempt} data-expected-output="" />
        <p class="output-note">{exampleNote}</p>
        <p>
          After every turn you'll see <code>messages</code>: your words, its tool calls, the results you handed back,
          its answer. There's no hidden state and no memory anywhere else. The whole agent is that list and this loop:
        </p>
        <CodeBlock client:load code={loopCode} language="ts" title={`the shape of the loop in ${file}`} copy={false} data-copy-exempt="a sketch of the loop to read; your coding agent writes the real file" />
        <p><strong class="text-bone">That's it. That's the agent.</strong></p>

        <h2 id="handoff">Hand it the keyboard</h2>
        <p>
          Everything so far, you built through a coding agent. You don't need it now. You have one.
        </p>
        <p>
          Run <code>{run}</code> and talk to yours instead. It can read <code>{file}</code>. It can change
          <code>{file}</code>. A few minutes ago it couldn't find a word in a file. Start by letting it name itself:
        </p>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your agent" prompt={promptName} /></div>
        <CodeBlock client:load code={outName} language="text" title="What you'll see" copy={false} data-copy-exempt={outputExempt} data-expected-output="" />
        <p class="output-note">Example: its name, and its words, will vary.</p>
        <p>
          Press <Kbd client:visible keys={["ctrl", "c"]} />, run it again, and say hello. It just edited its own code,
          and committed it. If it ever breaks itself, your coding agent is still there, every step is in git, and the
          finished <a href={finished}>{file}</a> is here.
        </p>
        <p><strong class="text-bone">From this moment on, you're talking to your agent.</strong></p>
        <p>It's real, and it's small. Before you trust it with anything that matters, here's what it doesn't have yet:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Memory.</strong> Quit, and it forgets everything. <code>messages</code> lives only as long as the process.</li>
          <li><strong>Safety.</strong> It runs any command it likes, in your folder, as you.</li>
          <li><strong>Compaction.</strong> Every turn makes <code>messages</code> longer. One day it won't fit in the model's context, and the agent stops working.</li>
          <li><strong>Recovery.</strong> If the network drops or the model errors, it crashes.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Most of a production agent is answers to those four. The loop stays the same.</p>

        <h2 id="keep-growing">Keep growing</h2>
        <p>From here, every change is a conversation with your agent. Restart it after each one so it runs as its new self.</p>

        <h3>A new tool</h3>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your agent" prompt={growList} /></div>
        <p>Restart and ask what's in the folder. It just gave itself a new ability.</p>

        <h3>Nicer to use</h3>
        <p>Replies arrive all at once, and errors crash it. Fix both, and watch the words stream in.</p>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your agent" prompt={growStream} /></div>

        <h3>Tidy tools</h3>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your agent" prompt={growRegistry} /></div>
        <p>Watch it refactor itself. Restart, and check it still works.</p>

        <h3>A sense of purpose</h3>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your agent" prompt={growPurpose} /></div>
        <p>Restart. This is where it stops being a demo and starts being yours.</p>

        <h3>Skills</h3>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your agent" prompt={growSkills} /></div>
        <p>Restart, then try:</p>
        <CodeBlock client:load code={termSkills} language="text" title="your agent" copyLabel="Copy these lines" />
        <p>
          Notice what a skill is: a markdown file. Your agent could already read one with <code>read_file</code>. The
          new tools just make the habit easy.
        </p>

        <h3>Skills that build tools</h3>
        <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your agent" prompt={growToolmaker} /></div>
        <p>
          A skill that teaches it to make tools, used to make a tool. Each growth makes the next one easier. Keep going:
          a web search tool, a git tool, a skill for writing tests, saving and resuming conversations.
        </p>

        <h2 id="sky">Sky's the limit</h2>
        <p>
          In February I was building a plugin for Slugger, my OpenClaw agent, and the plugin fought me at every turn. I
          wanted to understand the thing underneath, so Slugger and I wrote the first version of this guide. Then I
          followed it myself: I built the loop, about 150 lines of it, handed it the keyboard and kept going.
        </p>
        <p>
          That bootstrap became the Ouroboros harness. It runs <a href="/butler/">Butler</a>, which looks after my home
          server, and Shepherd, which keeps the agents in my terminals on task, every day. It's still where I try new
          harness ideas first. <a href="/desk/">Desk</a> grew out of the same exploration; it's a sibling, and it works
          with whichever harness you use. They're all on the <a href="/apps/">apps page</a>, and the whole path is in
          <a href="/lab/story/">the story</a>. To try the harness itself, start with
          <a href="/lab/docs/getting-started/">getting started</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          You opened this page with a secret word. You're leaving with something that can change itself. Mine became a
          harness. Yours can become anything.
        </p>

        <h2 id="side-quests">Side quests</h2>

        <Card as="section" id="bring-your-own-key" data-side-quest="" aria-label="Side quest: bring your own key" title="Bring your own key" description="Side quest" className="side-quest">
          <p>
            Your code only needs a key, an address and a model name. Any provider with an OpenAI-style chat completions
            API works:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>OpenAI:</strong> your OpenAI key, the address <code>https://api.openai.com/v1</code>, and a model like <code>gpt-4.1</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>MiniMax:</strong> your MiniMax key, the address <code>https://api.minimax.io/v1</code>, and a model like <code>MiniMax-M2.5</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Azure OpenAI:</strong> your resource's key, the address <code>https://your-resource.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/</code>, and your deployment name as the model.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your coding agent" prompt={promptByok} /></div>
          <a slot="footer" href="#byok-signpost" class="hit">Back to the main path</a>
        </Card>

        <Card as="section" id="responses-api" data-side-quest="" aria-label="Side quest: newer GPT models" title="Newer GPT models" description="Side quest" className="side-quest">
          <p>
            Some newer OpenAI models only answer through the Responses API, and chat completions turns them away with
            a <code>400</code>. The loop is the same; the shapes change. You send a list of input items instead of
            <code>messages</code>, a tool call arrives as a <code>function_call</code> item, and you answer it with a
            <code>function_call_output</code> item carrying the same <code>call_id</code>.
          </p>
          <div class="my-6"><PromptCard label="Tell your coding agent" prompt={promptResponses} /></div>
          <a slot="footer" href="#troubleshooting" class="hit">Back to the main path</a>
        </Card>

        <Card as="section" id="think-tags" data-side-quest="" aria-label="Side quest: models that think out loud" title="Models that think out loud" description="Side quest" className="side-quest">
          <p>
            Some reasoning models put their thinking in the reply itself, between <code>&lt;think&gt;</code> and
            <code>&lt;/think&gt;</code>. It's harmless, just noisy. Ask your coding agent to hide it when printing, or to
            print it dimmed so you can watch the model reason. Some providers want the thinking sent back with the
            conversation, so check their docs before you drop it from <code>messages</code>.
          </p>
          <a slot="footer" href="#m1-try" class="hit">Back to the main path</a>
        </Card>

        <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
        {faq.map(({ q, a }) => (
          <>
            <h3>{q}</h3>
            <p>{a}</p>
          </>
        ))}

      </div>

    </div>
  </article>

</Layout>

